---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import { LogoAnimation } from "../../components/cave/intro";
import {
  GoldMineSolver,
  GoldMineGame,
} from "../../components/cave/greedy-gold-miner";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="How to Escape With the Most Gold"
  description="Find the richest escape route: the path that collects the most gold before reaching the exit"
  theme="dark"
>
  <!-- Slide 1: Intro text -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        You enter a gold mine with one goal: to become filthy rich and make it
        out alive. But there's a catch. Every step you take collects gold — and
        collapses the tunnel behind you. There's no going back. Luckily, there's
        another way out. So which route leaves you the richest by the time you
        escape?
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 2: Interactive solver -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Write a Python function that finds a path from start to exit. The
        default code uses BFS — it finds the shortest path. Can you modify it to
        find the longest one instead?
      </p>
    </aside>
    <GoldMineSolver client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 3: Playable visual -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        This new visual reuses the same dungeon and the same tileset, but turns
        it into a game. The miner earns one unit of gold per step, and the floor
        behind them disappears. That lets the audience feel the problem before
        returning to the algorithm.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <GoldMineGame client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 4: Logo -->
  <section id="intro">
    <LogoAnimation client:load />
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
